Memory Addresses Types
	Logical: reference to a memory location independent of the current assignment of
		data to memory
	Relative: reference to memory address but with a reference to some known point
	Physical / Absolute: actual location in memory

EEprom: Electrically erasable programmable read-only memmory

static relocation: developed by IBM: modifies second program on the fly, has a large overhead

dynamic relocation: solution gives each process its own address space as protection
	* different part of the physical memory
	* memory protection for multiprocessing
	* easy  to implement w/ hardware
	** every operation needs an addition and comparison operation to ensure origin location

swapping: swap process in and out of memory to a "backing store"
	* allows for multiple processes that would normally exceed physical memory size

backing store: large storage for all processes

Memory Placement Algorithms
	*Best-fit: chooses the block that is closest in size, slow, tiny useless holes
	*First-fit: begins to scan memory from the beginning and chooses the first block that 
		is large enough, simple and fast, COULD PRODUCE LARGE HOLES
	*Next-fit: begins to scan memory from location of the last placement and chooses
		the next available block that is large enough
	*Worst-fit: to get around breaking up nearly exact matches into a process and a tiny hole
		    one could think about taking the largest hole available.
			*proved to be ineffective

internal fragmentation: wasted memory space due to the block of data loaded being
			smaller than the partition

external fragmentation: there is enough total unused memory for the new process
			but it is not contiguous, since it is fragmented into lots of small holes


